Did You Know?

   The apparat in apparatchik (a term English speakers borrowed from Russian)
   essentially means "party machine," with machine referring to a highly organized
   political group under the leadership of a boss or small group of individuals:
   apparatchik originally referred to someone functioning as a cog in the system of
   the Communist Party. The term is not a flattering one, and its negative
   connotations reflect the perception of some Communists as obedient drones in the
   great Party machine. In current use, however, a person doesn't have to be a
   member of the Communist Party to be called an apparatchik; they just have to be
   someone who mindlessly follows orders in an organization or bureaucracy.
